{"type": "step", "node_id": 23, "parent_node_id": 22, "role": "assistant", "content": "## Solution\n\nThe robot rolls a fair six-sided die repeatedly, accumulating a running total $S$. It stops as soon as $S > 20$. The final total must therefore lie in $\\{21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26\\}$, since the last roll adds between 1 and 6 to a total that was $\\le 20$.\n\n**Setup.** Let $g(s)$ denote the probability that the running total ever equals exactly $s$ (with $s \\le 20$, so the robot continues). Then $g(0)=1$, and for $s\\ge 1$:\n\n$$g(s) = \\frac{1}{6}\\sum_{r=1}^{6} g(s-r), \\qquad g(n)=0 \\text{ for } n<0.$$\n\nThe probability that the final total equals $k$ (for $k\\in\\{21,\\dots,26\\}$) is:\n\n$$P(k) = \\frac{1}{6}\\sum_{s=k-6}^{20} g(s),$$\n\nsince the robot must be at some state $s\\le 20$ and then roll exactly $k-s \\in \\{1,\\dots,6\\}$.\n\n**Computing $g(s)$.** For $s\\le 6$, the sum includes all of $g(0),\\dots,g(s-1)$, giving the closed form $g(s) = 7^{s-1}/6^s$ for $s\\ge 1$ (verified by induction using the geometric sum). For $s>6$, the six-term recurrence applies. Computing numerically through $s=20$:\n\n| $s$ | $g(s)$ | | $s$ | $g(s)$ |\n|-----|--------|---|-----|--------|\n| 0 | 1.0000 | | 11 | 0.2934 |\n| 1 | 0.1667 | | 12 | 0.2908 |\n| 2 | 0.1944 | | 13 | 0.2793 |\n| 3 | 0.2269 | | 14 | 0.2835 |\n| 4 | 0.2647 | | 15 | 0.2861 |\n| 5 | 0.3088 | | 16 | 0.2871 |\n| 6 | 0.3602 | | 17 | 0.2867 |\n| 7 | 0.2536 | | 18 | 0.2856 |\n| 8 | 0.2681 | | 19 | 0.2847 |\n| 9 | 0.2804 | | 20 | 0.2856 |\n| 10 | 0.2893 | | | |\n\n**Final probabilities.**\n\n| $k$ | $P(k)$ |\n|-----|--------|\n| 21 | $\\frac{1}{6}(g(15)+\\cdots+g(20)) \\approx 0.2860$ |\n| 22 | $\\frac{1}{6}(g(16)+\\cdots+g(20)) \\approx 0.2383$ |\n| 23 | $\\frac{1}{6}(g(17)+\\cdots+g(20)) \\approx 0.1904$ |\n| 24 | $\\frac{1}{6}(g(18)+g(19)+g(20)) \\approx 0.1427$ |\n| 25 | $\\frac{1}{6}(g(19)+g(20)) \\approx 0.0951$ |\n| 26 | $\\frac{1}{6}g(20) \\approx 0.0476$ |\n\nThese sum to $\\approx 1.0000$, confirming correctness.\n\n**Why 21 wins.** The values $g(15),\\dots,g(20)$ are all nearly equal ($\\approx 0.285$–$0.287$), since the distribution of the running total near 20 has stabilized. The probability $P(k)$ is proportional to the *number* of starting states $s\\in\\{k-6,\\dots,20\\}$ that can reach $k$, weighted by roughly equal $g(s)$ values. The final total $21$ can be reached from all six states $\\{15,16,17,18,19,20\\}$, while each larger $k$ loses one starting state. Hence $P(21) > P(22) > \\cdots > P(26)$, strictly decreasing.\n\n**The most probable final total distance is $\\boxed{21}$.**\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "Let me solve this problem. The robot rolls a die repeatedly, accumulating sum, stops when sum > 20. I need to find the most probable final total distance.\n\nLet me think about this carefully. The final total is in range 21-26 (since we exceed 20 by rolling a die that adds 1-6, and we were at ≤20 before).\n\nLet me compute the probability that the final sum equals each value from 21 to 26.\n\nLet f(s) = probability of reaching sum exactly s (and continuing, i.e., s ≤ 20). Actually let me define p(s) = probability that the robot ever has total exactly s, for s ≤ 20, and the process continues from there.\n\nLet me define g(s) = probability that the running total equals s at some point (with s ≤ 20). Then the probability that final total = k (for k in 21..26) is sum over s in [max(0,k-6), 20] of g(s) * (1/6) where the roll from s lands exactly on k, i.e., roll = k - s.\n\nActually g(s) is the probability the running total hits exactly s (and s ≤ 20 so it continues). g(0) = 1. For s ≥ 1, g(s) = (1/6) * sum_{r=1}^{6} g(s-r) where g(negative) = 0.\n\nThen P(final = k) = (1/6) * sum_{s=max(0,k-6)}^{20} g(s) for k in 21..26, where the roll k-s must be in 1..6, i.e., s in [k-6, k-1] ∩ [0,20]. Since k ≥ 21, k-1 ≥ 20, so s ranges from k-6 to 20.\n\nLet me compute g(s) for s = 0 to 20.\n\ng(0) = 1\ng(1) = (1/6)(g(0)) = 1/6\ng(2) = (1/6)(g(1)+g(0)) = (1/6)(1/6+1) = (1/6)(7/6) = 7/36\ng(3) = (1/6)(g(2)+g(1)+g(0)) = (1/6)(7/36 + 1/6 + 1) = (1/6)(7/36 + 6/36 + 36/36) = (1/6)(49/36) = 49/216\ng(4) = (1/6)(g(3)+g(2)+g(1)+g(0)) = (1/6)(49/216 + 7/36 + 1/6 + 1)\n\nLet me convert: 49/216 + 42/216 + 36/216 + 216/216 = 343/216. So g(4) = 343/1296.\n\nI see a pattern: g(s) = 7^s / 6^(s+1) for s = 0,1,2,3,4? Let me check:\ng(0) = 1 = 7^0/6^1 = 1/6? No, that's 1/6 not 1. Hmm.\n\nActually g(0)=1. g(1)=1/6. g(2)=7/36. g(3)=49/216. g(4)=343/1296.\n\nFor s ≥ 1: g(s) = 7^(s-1)/6^s. Check: g(1) = 7^0/6^1 = 1/6 ✓. g(2) = 7^1/6^2 = 7/36 ✓. g(3) = 7^2/6^3 = 49/216 ✓. g(4) = 7^3/6^4 = 343/1296 ✓.\n\nThis pattern holds as long as s ≤ 6, because for s ≤ 6, all g(s-r) for r=1..6 with s-r ≥ 0 are included, and the sum g(s-1)+...+g(0). Let me verify the recurrence gives this.\n\nFor s ≤ 6: g(s) = (1/6) sum_{j=0}^{s-1} g(j). With g(0)=1, g(j) = 7^{j-1}/6^j for j≥1.\n\nsum_{j=0}^{s-1} g(j) = 1 + sum_{j=1}^{s-1} 7^{j-1}/6^j = 1 + (1/6) sum_{j=1}^{s-1} (7/6)^{j-1} = 1 + (1/6) * ((7/6)^{s-1} - 1)/(7/6 - 1) = 1 + (1/6) * ((7/6)^{s-1}-1)/(1/6) = 1 + ((7/6)^{s-1} - 1) = (7/6)^{s-1}.\n\nSo g(s) = (1/6)(7/6)^{s-1} = 7^{s-1}/6^s. ✓ for s ≥ 1, s ≤ 6.\n\nFor s > 6, the recurrence is g(s) = (1/6) sum_{r=1}^{6} g(s-r), i.e., g(s) = (1/6)(g(s-1)+g(s-2)+g(s-3)+g(s-4)+g(s-5)+g(s-6)).\n\nLet me compute numerically. Let me use decimals.\n\ng(0) = 1\ng(1) = 0.16666667\ng(2) = 0.19444444\ng(3) = 0.22685185\ng(4) = 0.26466049\ng(5) = 0.30877057\ng(6) = 0.36023234\n\nLet me verify g(6) = 7^5/6^6 = 16807/46656 = 0.3602318... ✓\n\ng(7) = (1/6)(g(6)+g(5)+g(4)+g(3)+g(2)+g(1))\n= (1/6)(0.36023234 + 0.30877057 + 0.26466049 + 0.22685185 + 0.19444444 + 0.16666667)\n= (1/6)(1.52162636)\n= 0.25360439\n\ng(8) = (1/6)(g(7)+g(6)+g(5)+g(4)+g(3)+g(2))\n= (1/6)(0.25360439 + 0.36023234 + 0.30877057 + 0.26466049 + 0.22685185 + 0.19444444)\n= (1/6)(1.60856408)\n= 0.26809401\n\ng(9) = (1/6)(g(8)+g(7)+g(6)+g(5)+g(4)+g(3))\n= (1/6)(0.26809401 + 0.25360439 + 0.36023234 + 0.30877057 + 0.26466049 + 0.22685185)\n= (1/6)(1.68221365)\n= 0.28036894\n\ng(10) = (1/6)(g(9)+g(8)+g(7)+g(6)+g(5)+g(4))\n= (1/6)(0.28036894 + 0.26809401 + 0.25360439 + 0.36023234 + 0.30877057 + 0.26466049)\n= (1/6)(1.73573074)\n= 0.28928846\n\ng(11) = (1/6)(g(10)+g(9)+g(8)+g(7)+g(6)+g(5))\n= (1/6)(0.28928846 + 0.28036894 + 0.26809401 + 0.25360439 + 0.36023234 + 0.30877057)\n= (1/6)(1.76035871)\n= 0.29339312\n\ng(12) = (1/6)(g(11)+g(10)+g(9)+g(8)+g(7)+g(6))\n= (1/6)(0.29339312 + 0.28928846 + 0.28036894 + 0.26809401 + 0.25360439 + 0.36023234)\n= (1/6)(1.74498126)\n= 0.29083021\n\ng(13) = (1/6)(g(12)+g(11)+g(10)+g(9)+g(8)+g(7))\n= (1/6)(0.29083021 + 0.29339312 + 0.28928846 + 0.28036894 + 0.26809401 + 0.25360439)\n= (1/6)(1.67557913)\n= 0.27926319\n\ng(14) = (1/6)(g(13)+g(12)+g(11)+g(10)+g(9)+g(8))\n= (1/6)(0.27926319 + 0.29083021 + 0.29339312 + 0.28928846 + 0.28036894 + 0.26809401)\n= (1/6)(1.70123793)\n= 0.28353966\n\ng(15) = (1/6)(g(14)+g(13)+g(12)+g(11)+g(10)+g(9))\n= (1/6)(0.28353966 + 0.27926319 + 0.29083021 + 0.29339312 + 0.28928846 + 0.28036894)\n= (1/6)(1.71668358)\n= 0.28611393\n\ng(16) = (1/6)(g(15)+g(14)+g(13)+g(12)+g(11)+g(10))\n= (1/6)(0.28611393 + 0.28353966 + 0.27926319 + 0.29083021 + 0.29339312 + 0.28928846)\n= (1/6)(1.72242857)\n= 0.28707143\n\ng(17) = (1/6)(g(16)+g(15)+g(14)+g(13)+g(12)+g(11))\n= (1/6)(0.28707143 + 0.28611393 + 0.28353966 + 0.27926319 + 0.29083021 + 0.29339312)\n= (1/6)(1.72021154)\n= 0.28670192\n\ng(18) = (1/6)(g(17)+g(16)+g(15)+g(14)+g(13)+g(12))\n= (1/6)(0.28670192 + 0.28707143 + 0.28611393 + 0.28353966 + 0.27926319 + 0.29083021)\n= (1/6)(1.71352034)\n= 0.28558672\n\ng(19) = (1/6)(g(18)+g(17)+g(16)+g(15)+g(14)+g(13))\n= (1/6)(0.28558672 + 0.28670192 + 0.28707143 + 0.28611393 + 0.28353966 + 0.27926319)\n= (1/6)(1.70827685)\n= 0.28471281\n\ng(20) = (1/6)(g(19)+g(18)+g(17)+g(16)+g(15)+g(14))\n= (1/6)(0.28471281 + 0.28558672 + 0.28670192 + 0.28707143 + 0.28611393 + 0.28353966)\n= (1/6)(1.71372647)\n= 0.28562108\n\nNow P(final = k) = (1/6) * sum_